----------------------------------------	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
--------------------		Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
----------------------------------------	    // it corresponds with the precondition of 'arrList.Contains(x)'
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
--------------------	    // to meet this condition an index from 0 to arrList.count should be tested.
	    // "@@@" should not changed by the user and the tool should insert "\(\)".

            Assert.AreEqual(arrList[index], x);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.IndexOf(x) == index);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.LastIndexOf(x) == index);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

            arrList[index] = arrList[index - 1];

            Assert.AreEqual(arrList[index], Old_x);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.IndexOf(x) != index - 1);                  
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.LastIndexOf(x) == index);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
        }


A: For any assertion or condition testing that you want to use Debug.Assert(...);, you need to find a way to make a valid condition for it. In this example, we assume that you want to test whether the "index of x in old array list" is greater than or equal to -1. The condition should be tested for an index from 0 to arrList.count. The "@@@" should not be changed by the user and the tool should insert "\(\)". It may look like this:
--------------------	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
------------------------------------------------------------